No, Section 200 of the Indian Evidence Act, 1872 does not allow a judge to ignore all witness testimony if he personally knows the facts. If a Judge personally knows the facts of any case, he shall not give any evidence thereon, except in the following cases—

		When he is required by law to disclose any confidential communication made to him in his professional capacity as such;
		When he is acting as a witness, or taking part in any inquiry or proceeding, as a witness;
		When he is acting as a witness in a suit in which another party to the suit is acting in good faith as his agent;
		When he is acting as a witness in a suit upon a promissory note or bill of exchange, or in a proceeding to execute or perfect the same;
		When he is acting as a witness in a suit or proceeding for the recovery of money out of his own hands;
